  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 446 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe 00Z RAOB data show the deep trough responsible for this week's \nheavy rainfall stretching from the Great Lakes into the Mid-South, \ncarrying the storm system and its moisture axis east of the area. WV \nimagery suggests a dry, subsident northwest flow over the entire CWA \nwith surface high pressure building in today, helping to push the \nlast of the remnant showers east of the area before daybreak or \nshortly after. The airmass change will finally bring a fall-like feel\nto the area with afternoon dewpoints falling into the 40s today. The\nrain is finished, but recovery from the flooding lingers as area \nrivers will take much of the weekend to recede.\n\nThe weekend will be quiet and crisp, very fall-like. Highs hold\nnear 70 both days with overnight lows in the 40s, light winds, and\nwide diurnal temperature swings under mostly clear skies. The\npotential for fog can't yet be ruled out but looks overall low:\ntemperature-dewpoint spreads generally narrow around sunrise with\nlight winds over ground still saturated from the flooding, so if\npatchy fog does form it would likely be focused in river valleys\nand low-lying spots, especially Saturday morning with the surface\nridge centered across the area. A reinforcing dry push Sunday\nnight keeps Monday seasonably cool with another morning in the\n40s.\n\nIn the extended period, the upper ridge amplifies over the Western\nCONUS this weekend and spreads east across the Plains next week,\nand every WPC cluster through the end of next week keeps positive\nheight anomalies centered over the central CONUS, and no cluster\nbrings a meaningful weather system through the area. Ensemble\nprecipitation stays near zero through the period and highs climb\nsteadily, reaching near 80 by Wednesday with lower 80s possible\nlate in the week, which is around to slightly above normal for\nthis time of year. Breezier south winds return by late week as the\nridge axis shifts east and the gradient tightens across the region\nwith a lee trough deepening. Slight thunderstorm signals toward\nthe end of the period reflect growing ensemble spread rather than\nany organized system impacting the region. Confidence in the dry,\nslightly warming forecast through the upcoming week is high.",
